B.VZ=new A.w(B.a_8,["A battery stores chemical reactants. As current flows, chemicals react until exhausted. Once all chemical reactants are used up, the battery can no longer push electrons and its voltage drops to 0V.","Voltage is electrical push pressure! A 9V battery pushes electrons 6 times harder than a 1.5V AA cell, making it great for driving Arduino boards and motors together.","That is a short circuit! Resistance is nearly 0 \u03a9, causing massive current surge that can make wires smoke, heat up, or damage the battery. Always put a load (resistor, motor, LED) in the loop!","Batteries provide Direct Current (DC)! Electrons always flow steadily in one direction from negative to positive."],t.w)
B.W8=new A.w(B.a9,["Battery (DC Power Source)","How does a battery push electrons through a circuit using chemical energy?","A battery is an electrochemical power source that stores chemical energy and converts it into electrical potential (voltage). It maintains a positive anode (+) and negative cathode (\u2212), creating a direct current (DC) push that drives electrons through closed circuit loops.","Real-Life Memory Analogy: Think of a battery like a pressurized rooftop water pump! The pump forces water up to high pressure (Voltage), sending it rushing down pipes to turn waterwheels, before returning to the ground reservoir.","Cause & Effect: Why do we need a battery? Without a voltage source, electrons stay stationary (0V = 0 mA current). When connected, chemical potential drives steady current to energize sensors, LEDs, and motors. Reverse polarity can burn sensitive chips!","Real-World Everyday Places: Inside smartphones, Mars rovers, electric cars (EV battery packs), drones, handheld game consoles, and robot rovers.","When to Use It: Whenever your robot needs untethered portable power without a wall cable, or when providing regulated 5V/3.3V logic levels to microcontrollers.",B.S1,B.RZ,B.VZ],t.R)
