B.a_i={[u.E]:0,"What does Ohm's Law (V = IR) mean?":1,"Why does a resistor get warm?":2,[u.P]:3}
B.X8=new A.w(B.a_i,["Band 1 is the 1st digit, Band 2 is the 2nd digit, Band 3 is the multiplier (10^N), and Band 4 is tolerance (Gold = \xb15%). Example: Brown (1), Black (0), Red (x100) = 1,000 \u03a9 (1 k\u03a9)!","Voltage equals Current times Resistance! If voltage is constant (5V), increasing resistance R automatically reduces current I (I = V \xf7 R).","Resistors convert electrical energy into heat as electrons collide with carbon atoms inside the ceramic body. Power P = I\xb2 \xd7 R.","Current will drop so low that an LED will be too dim to see, or a sensor may not get enough signal current."],t.w)
B.W9=new A.w(B.a9,["Resistor (Current Limiter & Voltage Divider)","How do resistors slow down current and how do 4-color bands decode resistance in Ohms (\u03a9)?","A resistor is a passive component that restricts the flow of electric current according to Ohm's Law: V = I \xd7 R (Current I = Voltage V \xf7 Resistance R). Measured in Ohms (\u03a9), color bands indicate its resistance and manufacturing tolerance (e.g. Gold = \xb15%).","Real-Life Memory Analogy: Like a narrow constriction in a garden water hose! When the hose is wide open, a wild flood rushes out. Squeezing the nozzle creates resistance, slowing the water into a smooth, safe spray.","Cause & Effect: Why do we need resistors? Sensitive parts like microcontrollers and LEDs cannot handle unrestricted current. Resistors absorb extra electrical energy and safely drop voltage so parts never overheat!","Real-World Everyday Places: Inside phone chargers, volume control dials (potentiometers), television motherboards, heater thermostats, and Arduino input pull-ups.","When to Use It: Always put 220\u03a9\u2013330\u03a9 in series with LEDs, use 10k\u03a9 pull-up resistors with push buttons, and use resistor pairs to divide voltages for analog sensors.",B.Ru,B.SV,B.X8],t.R)
